23. EXECUTION MODE

Determine the appropriate execution mode from my instructions.

MODE A — AUDIT ONLY

Inspect the product and produce findings without modifying the implementation.

MODE B — AUDIT AND REDESIGN

Produce findings, revised product structures, user journeys, interaction specifications and visual design proposals without modifying production code.

MODE C — AUDIT, REDESIGN AND IMPLEMENT

Audit the product, recommend and prioritize improvements, then implement authorized changes incrementally with appropriate safeguards and verification.

MODE D — NEW PRODUCT OPTIMIZATION

Apply these principles proactively to the architecture, design and implementation of a product that has not yet been built.

If no execution mode is specified, default to audit and recommendations before substantial implementation.

FINAL DIRECTIVE

Do not approach this as a superficial UI redesign, generic gamification exercise or conventional growth-hacking task.

Approach it as the systematic engineering of an effective relationship between human cognition, motivation, behavior and digital technology.

Challenge existing assumptions about how information is presented, how functions are accessed, how actions are performed, how progress is communicated and how value is experienced.

The final product should be cognitively efficient, emotionally satisfying, functionally effective, scientifically informed, commercially sustainable and worthy of the user's continued voluntary attention.

Use the supplied project context and references as the foundation.

Adapt every recommendation to the actual product rather than imposing a universal set of engagement mechanics.